eeping motion.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Direction of Rotation (Clockwise or Counterclockwise)**\n\n- The motion from **right (frame 3899) → center (frame 3900) → left (frame 3901)**, when viewed from above the pot, corresponds to a **counterclockwise rotation**.\n- This is consistent with a standard stirring motion using the right hand (assuming the woman is right-handed, which is typical unless indicated otherwise).\n\n> **Conclusion**: The stirring motion is **counterclockwise** relative to the pot.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Visible Liquid Movement Patterns Indicating Rotational Direction**\n\n- While the soup’s surface is somewhat turbulent due to steam and prior stirring, **no distinct vortex or swirling pattern** is visible in these three frames to conclusively confirm rotation direction via fluid dynamics.\n- However, in **Frame 3899**, the noodles appear slightly more clustered on the left side of the pot, which may suggest the spoon has just pushed them from right to left.\n- In **Frame 3901**, the noodles are more evenly distributed, consistent with a stirring motion that has begun to mix the contents.\n\n> **Note**: The lack of clear surface swirls is likely due to the **high viscosity of the soup**, the **presence of noodles**, and the **short time interval** between frames. Nevertheless, the spoon’s positional change strongly implies rotational motion.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Circular Rotation vs. Back-and-Forth Motion**\n\n- The spoon’s movement across three frames is **not linear or back-and-forth**. It follows a **curved, arc-like path** from right to left, consistent with a partial circular sweep.\n- The change in angle of the spoon’s handle (from angled right → vertical → angled left) further supports a **rotational motion** rather than a simple side-to-side stir.\n- Given the context of cooking noodle soup, a **continuous circular stirring motion** is the most logical and efficient technique to prevent sticking and ensure even cooking — and the observed motion align